#X text 16 22 Now we have [lop3~] as the filter inside the feedback loop. This is a one pole / one zero filter which allows us to set a cutoff frequency with high accuracy. You can also use [lop~] if you will \, but I feel it's too cheap for this algorithm. As promised \, now we have the feedback loop and filter inside a subpatch with a block size of 1 \, which allows very high pitches!, f 60;
#X obj 80 412 brown~;
#X obj 660 409 hsl 128 15 -0.99999 0.99999 0 0 empty fb empty -2 -6 0 8 #dcdcdc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 657 433 8 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 695 408 feedback;
#X floatatom 600 368 10 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 718 468 hsl 128 15 200 20000 1 0 empty cutoff empty -2 -6 0 8 #dcdcdc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 715 492 11 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 753 467 cutoff;
#X obj 543 578 out~;
#X obj 600 315 rand.i 25 50;
#X obj 600 341 mtof;
#X obj 600 251 tgl 19 0 empty empty empty 0 -10 0 12 #dfdfdf #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X obj 600 289 tempo 100;
#X obj 564 286 bng 21 250 50 0 empty empty empty 0 -10 0 12 #dfdfdf #000000 #000000;
#X obj 543 402 pink~;
#X text 16 135 Another variation is to use another excitation/noise source such as pink or brown noise \, both used here. Remember that brown noise has 6db decay in energy per octave \, and pink has 3db. Hence \, using them sounds less harsh than white noise and it's like having a pre filter. You can even try other arbitrary sources \, of course.;
#N canvas 138 71 511 556 Feedback_Lowpass_1st 0;
#X obj 165 58 inlet;
